One can also compute \(\sigma\) from non-equilibrium MD simulations under external electric fields. Figure 3c shows the total displacement of charges in the system over time under different values of the external field \(\mathcal{E}^{0}\) along the \(z\) direction, \(D(t)=\sum_{i}^{N} q_{i}\left(z_{i}(t)-z_{i}(0)\right)\), computed using the constant charges \(q^{H}=+1\) and \(q^{O}=-2 . \sigma\) can be estimated from the slope of \(D(t)\) as \(\sigma=\langle d D(t) / d t\rangle / V \mathcal{E}^{0}\). Such \(\sigma\) values from these finite-electric-field simulations, as displayed in the legend of Fig. 3c, are consistent with the equilibrium results, but have better statistical convergence and also avoid the problems associated with the Green-Kubo integration to the infinite time limit (Eq. (11)).

\section*{Example: Ferroelectric perovskite}

Ferroelectric materials are unique in that they exhibit spontaneous and permanent electric polarization, and this polarization can be reversed by applying an electric field \({ }^{54}\). Anomalously large BECs that exceed the nominal charges of ions are often considered hallmarks of ferroelectric materials \({ }^{55-57}\). Here, we demonstrate that our method can predict the anomalous BECs and model the characteristic \(P-\mathcal{E}\) hysteresis loop in the prototypical \(\mathrm{PbTiO}_{3}\) ferroelectric perovskite. At \(T=300 \mathrm{~K}, \mathrm{PbTiO}_{3}\) exhibits a tetragonal phase characterized by a short axis \(a\) and a long axis \(c\), with the ratio \(c / a\) correlated with the polarization magnitude (see Fig. 4a).

We trained the CACE-LR model using the energies and forces of the \(\mathrm{PbTiO}_{3}\) dataset from ref. 58, computed using SCAN DFT. The potential achieved test RMSEs of 0.4 meV /atom in energy and \(79.8 \mathrm{meV} / \AA\) in forces, much reduced from the original model with 1 meV /atom in energy RMSE and \(350 \mathrm{meV} / \AA\) in force RMSE from ref. 58.

Without explicitly learning the BEC tensors, Fig. 4b compares them for 45 atomic configurations (including both cubic and tetragonal phase) predicted using LES and computed using PBE DFT. Not only do the LES predictions agree well with the DFT reference, the anomalously large diagonal values of BECs ( \(Z_{\alpha \alpha}^{*}\) ) relative to the nominal ionic charges (indicated by the plus signs in Fig. 4b) for \(q^{\mathrm{Pb}}=+2, q^{\mathrm{Ti}}=+4\), and \(q^{\mathrm{O}}=-2\) are captured. The anomalous diagonal BECs in ferroelectrics are typically

